From 2b0a7021369a66eb0ff1c9fb2e1ad946a3b270e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 03:27:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: on a dual-mode DP connector, native Dolby
 Vision left toward a GUI of another size turns the output off and on once

On a dual-mode DisplayPort connector that nvidia-drm drives as HDMI, every
change of mode goes out with the head turned off and on, and since the
previous commit a change of resolution does too. Leaving native Dolby Vision
toward a mode of another window size (issue #4: the GUI at 1920x1080 at
120 Hz, a video in Dolby Vision at 3840x2160 at 23.976 Hz) then turned the
head off and on twice in a row: the restoration of the ordinary output went
out on its own, in the mode of the video, with the head off and on
(FlipDVBridgePage()), and the change of mode after it, from scratch, again.
The restoration joined with the next mode (CModeChangeHold::DVRestoreJoins())
is only for a mode of the same window size: a new window makes a new
surface, whose buffers the old mode cannot show.

There the restoration now waits for the new window: the end of a video waits
for the change of mode, as for one of the same size (DVEndWaitsForMode());
CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::SetFullScreen() makes the window with native output
still captured (DestroyWindow() leaves the restoration to it), and then
restores with the first frame of that window, black, the head turned off with
the new mode and on again (RestoreDVInNewWindow(), EndDVBridge() with the
mode). The change of mode after it finds that mode on the output and goes out
in place. If that does not go through, the ordinary output is restored on its
own in the new mode, and after that once a second, as after any refusal.

The rule is CModeChangeHold::DVRestoreInNewWindow(): the output captured,
atomic and on, another mode that needs another window size, not 3D, a link
that starts from scratch for native output and for a change of mode
(video/dualmoderestart on). An HDMI connector, a DisplayPort display, the
setting off, or a mode of the same size are left as they were. The decision
of FlipDVBridgePage() on turning the head off and on to restore is
NVIDIA_DUAL_MODE::RestoreRestarts(), the same expression, so that a host test
counts the times the head is turned off and on for each case.

Not run on a graphics card: the decisions are tested on the host, the
sequence in SetFullScreen() is read and compiled.
